    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## The membership-level cap\n\nThe rule \u2014 *a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum among their household members; no cap when there is no existing member* \u2014 I placed as a stored ceiling resolved at creation time, since the \"derived\" level (from points/tenure) drifts over time but the cap must stay fixed to what the household looked like when the owner joined.\n\n- **`Owner`** (`model/Owner.java`): added a stored `membershipLevelCap` field (+ column in all four `schema.sql` dialects). `getMembershipLevel()` now computes the derived 1\u20134 level and returns `min(derived, cap)`; a `null` cap means uncapped. The point\u2192level math was extracted to a private `derivedMembershipLevel()` so the getter has a single job: apply the ceiling.\n- **`HouseholdMembers`** (new): the single place that resolves \"the existing owners in this owner's household,\" keyed via the existing `HouseholdKey`.\n- **`HouseholdSizeCalculator`**: refactored to reuse `HouseholdMembers` (`size = existing + 1`) instead of duplicating the household-scan loop.\n- **`MembershipLevelCapCalculator`** (new): `one + max(member.getMembershipLevel())`, or `null` when there are no existing members.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.createOwner`**: sets the cap (alongside the other create-time derivations) before saving.\n\n## One necessary correction\n\nThe test creates a second owner in the same household (same last name + postcode, different telephone/email, *without* declaring `sharesHousehold`) and expects it to succeed. That was being rejected with `409 DuplicateOwnerException`.\n\n`DuplicateOwnerValidator` was rejecting on the **household key** alone, which contradicts three documented contracts in the same codebase: `Owner.getIdentityKey` (\"housemates \u2026 with different telephones \u2026 are both allowed\"), `ExceptionControllerAdvice` (\"thrown when \u2026 the whole identity key \u2026 matches\"), and the very existence of `PossibleDuplicateDetector` (which flags same-household/different-telephone owners as allowed-but-flagged \u2014 dead code under the old rule). I aligned the validator with its documented contract: it now rejects only on an exact **identity-key** match, so genuine hard duplicates are still blocked while legitimate housemates are permitted (and flagged). This is what makes multi-member households \u2014 and therefore the cap \u2014 reachable at all.\n\n`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u00b7 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
